WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:04.189
Old school vlogbrothers video where I sit
down with no idea what I'm going to talk about..

00:00:04.189 --> 00:00:07.209
about, what I'm going to talk - whatever,
sometimes you need to end a sentence with

00:00:07.209 --> 00:00:08.209
a preposition.

00:00:08.209 --> 00:00:10.150
Okay, so a while ago I was getting dressed
for a cocktail party and I was putting on

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.731
like, proper fancy pants and everything, and
I was looking at myself in the mirror, and

00:00:12.731 --> 00:00:16.700
I said to Sarah, "I feel like I look like
a middle aged man in this outfit".

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.920
And then Sarah paused for like a quarter second
too long before saying, "Nonono you look great!"

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.460
And I realized, I look like a middle aged
man, because I am a middle aged man.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.750
Like if I die at double my current age, people
won't be like, "Oh, it's a tragedy", they'll

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.430
be like, "Nyehh, he had a good run.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.690
Coulda been better, wasn't terrible".

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.569
My theory about middle age is that if you
triple your current age, and that's like a

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:40.519
reasonable age for a human being to be, then
you're not yet middle aged.

00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:43.829
I mean Hank you're 33, you triple that you're
99, that's right on the edge.

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:46.139
I'm 36, you're triple that, that's 108.

00:00:46.139 --> 00:00:48.780
Living to 108 doesn't even seem like a good
outcome to me.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.609
Anyway, there are tons of obviously negative
things about being middle aged, but there

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:56.539
are also some advantages, you know, like the
stability of adulthood, and the pleasure of

00:00:56.539 --> 00:00:57.539
parenting.

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:00.809
But the biggest thing, Hank, is that no one
expects me to know anything about pop culture,

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:03.079
which is great because I don't.

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:06.079
And this brings us, belatedly, to Justin Bieber.

00:01:06.079 --> 00:01:09.170
To be honest with you, Hank, I know this sounds
crazy, but I don't think I've ever heard a

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.180
Justin Bieber song.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.770
Actually, I'm gonna listen to like his top
5 songs right now, and see if I've ever heard

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:14.770
any of them.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:15.770
Hold on.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:16.770
Okay, this one's called "Boyfriend".

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.280
It has like, 3 hundred million views.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.310
I haven't heard this song before.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:22.310
It's kinda good.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.530
Is he a rapper?

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:25.940
It's a good song, I never heard it before
though.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.080
Alright, lets see the next one.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.250
Okay, this one's called uh, this one's called
"One Time".

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.930
Oh he looks much younger in this video.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.159
I kinda like this one as well.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:33.720
Although he just rhymed "knock" with "stop".

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.840
Yeah, I never heard that song before in my
life.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:36.840
Okay, let's see what's next.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.500
"Beauty and da Beat", featuring Nicki Minaj.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.500
Nope.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.710
Uh, I have never heard "Beauty and da Beat".

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.130
I'm only gonna try one more.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.619
It's the most viewed one ever.

00:01:45.619 --> 00:01:47.320
It's called "Baby" and it features Ludacris.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.780
This seems familiar to me.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.700
I feel like I have heard this song before.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.310
I feel like I've heard it, but I feel like
I've heard a cover of it or something?

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.999
Anyway, that one is definitely familiar.

00:01:54.999 --> 00:01:57.600
"Baby" featuring Ludacris is familiar to me.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.590
So there you have it, I've heard one Justin
Bieber song, I think.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:01.590
And yet.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.370
Despite my obvious and complete ignorance
of Justin Bieber and his work, I can tell

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:10.160
you that Justin Bieber was recently arrested
for DUI, driving a rented Lamborghini, and

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.610
in fact, I can tell you the color of that
Lamborghini: it was yellow.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:14.730
Why do I know this, Hank?

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.050
Non-rhetorical question.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.110
Why do I know about it?

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.421
I have no investment in Justin Bieber, I mean
I don't think he should drink and drive, but

00:02:21.421 --> 00:02:23.120
I don't think anyone should drink and drive.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.660
Why do I know the color of that Lamborghini?

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.310
Why do I have the image of Britney Spears'
shaved head imprinted upon my mind where I

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.420
might be remembering, I don't know, how to
speak Dutch or something.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.330
So Hank, I feel like a relatively small number
of people raises someone up to the level of

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:41.160
celebrity, and then everyone gets to participate
in like, relishing their downfall.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.080
It seems to me that Justin Bieber is now more
likely to be on the cover of tabloid magazines

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.660
than he was back when he was doing really
well, like in the "Baby" era--

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.660
OHHHHHHH!

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:51.660
"Baby"!

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:52.660
It's from your song Hank!

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.800
The "Ice Cream Changes" song, I know it because
of your song!

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.930
If you don't know what I'm talking about,
link in the Doobly-doo.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.870
But Hank, I haven't heard that Justin Bieber
song, I only heard your cover of it.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.310
I'm still zero for Bieber!

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.710
Or, I was, until I listened to those songs
anyway, which I quite liked.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:09.120
Hank, I don't want to excuse anyone's behaviour,
and I certainly don't want to act like celebrity

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.820
is the most troubling form of oppression out
there.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.760
But I do worry about the way we approach celebrities'
problems with relish.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.340
Not so much because it's bad for the celebrities,
but because I think it might be bad for us.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.170
I don't think relentless public chastising
much helps anyone.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:23.900
If it did, we would see this happen less,
and we aren't.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.060
Hank, it's really hard to choose what we pay
attention to, but I think in a lot of ways

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.290
it's also one of the most important choices
we make.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:34.060
So here's to sober driving, imagining others
complexly, and not paying attention to the

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.150
color of someone else's rented Lamborghini.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.680
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

